"Where is AI?"


Students identify AI systems they interact with every day and classify:

What the AI does

What data it uses

What business problem it solves

Who benefits

What could go wrong

The AI system I interact with most in computer science is AI agents such as Claude or Copilot. They directly access the application that you're working on and are able to see and change it for you. It solves a mostly convenience-based problem, where, for IDE's specifically, instead of having to copy all of your code over to the AI for context, it establishes a direct line of communication between your work/code and the AI. It benefits the user mostly, giving them convenient features and ease of access, while also allowing these AI companies to gather data and refine their algorithms. By allowing these AI to directly interface with your workspace, it can eliminate most, if not all of the work required from the user, which encourages lazy practices and anti-intellectualism. It encourages people to become more dependent on AI and less dependent on themselves.
